    <body>
      <sec id="article-26702.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The pars nervosa (neurohypophysis or posterior pituitary) is a neuroendocrine structure that, together with the anterior and intermediate lobes, constitutes the pituitary gland. The pars nervosa connects to the hypothalamus via the infundibular stalk and resides within the sella turcica, a saddle-shaped indentation of the sphenoid bone located superior to the nasopharynx.</p>
        <p>The structure functions in the storage and release of the neurohypophysial hormones oxytocin and arginine vasopressin (AVP; also antidiuretic&#x000a0;hormone or ADH), which are synthesized in hypothalamic neurons, into the systemic circulation. These hormones exert critical effects on blood pressure and osmolarity regulation, as well as on parturition and lactation.&#x000a0;Consequently, regulated secretion of&#x000a0;oxytocin and AVP represents the primary physiologic role of the pars nervosa (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Structural Components of the Pituitary Gland).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Pathology involving the pars nervosa can lead to&#x000a0;endocrine abnormalities,&#x000a0;such as diabetes insipidus, syndrome of inappropriate&#x000a0;ADH&#x000a0;(SIADH)&#x000a0;secretion, and&#x000a0;disorders&#x000a0;of parturition and lactation. Proximity of the posterior pituitary to the anterior pituitary and infundibular stalk poses surgical challenges, necessitating meticulous technique during transsphenoidal or transcranial procedures to minimize vascular and endocrine complications. Detailed knowledge of pars nervosa anatomy and function enables clinicians to interpret pituitary imaging accurately, anticipate&#x000a0;hormonal consequences of pituitary lesions or surgery, and guide effective management of neurohypophyseal disorders.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26702.s2" sec-type="Structure and Function">
        <title>Structure and Function</title>
        <p>The pars nervosa, together with the infundibular stalk, constitutes the posterior pituitary gland. The infundibular stalk contains unmyelinated axons of magnocellular neurosecretory cells that originate in the hypothalamus. These axons project to the pars nervosa, releasing the neuroendocrine hormones oxytocin and AVP into the systemic circulation through fenestrated sinusoidal capillaries. The pars nervosa is frequently regarded as an extension of the hypothalamus because&#x000a0;neuronal cell bodies in the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei synthesize these hormones. The 2 hormones released by the pars nervosa perform distinct physiologic functions.</p>
        <p>Oxytocin induces smooth muscle&#x000a0;contraction, facilitating uterine contraction during parturition.&#x000a0;This hormone also stimulates myoepithelial cells, promoting milk letdown during lactation.</p>
        <p>AVP, also known as ADH, mitigates the effects of volume depletion through multiple mechanisms mediated by specific receptor subtypes. The V1a receptor, expressed primarily on vascular smooth muscle, induces arteriolar contraction upon AVP stimulation. The V2 receptor, located on renal collecting ducts and tubular epithelium, promotes synthesis and membrane insertion of the water channel aquaporin-2, enhancing solute-free water reabsorption and reducing plasma osmolality. Coordinated activity of these receptors allows AVP to regulate blood tonicity and maintain blood pressure during states of volume loss.</p>
        <p>The V3, or V1b, receptor is expressed in various organs, including the anterior pituitary. In this region,&#x000a0;V1b receptor activation by AVP stimulates adrenocorticotropin release, often in conjunction with corticotropin-releasing hormone, and modulates the secretion of other pituitary peptides, such as &#x003b2;-endorphin and prolactin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>On routine histology, the pars nervosa resembles neural tissue more than a typical endocrine gland. The structure consists primarily of unmyelinated hypothalamo-hypophyseal axons and their terminals, scattered pituicytes (glial-like supporting cells), and a dense network of fenestrated capillaries. A characteristic feature is Herring bodies, focal axonal swellings containing neurosecretory granules with oxytocin or AVP bound to neurophysins, frequently observed near capillaries. Hematoxylin and eosin staining reveals a pale, fibrous background with numerous small, oval pituicyte nuclei, while individual axons are generally indistinct (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Microscopic Anatomy&#x000a0;of&#x000a0;the Hypophysis).</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26702.s3" sec-type="Embryology">
        <title>Embryology</title>
        <p>The pars nervosa originates from neuroectoderm, as does the remainder of the hypothalamus, in contrast to the anterior and intermediate lobes of the pituitary gland, which arise from migratory oral ectoderm known as the Rathke pouch. Between the 4th and 8th weeks of fetal development, a region of the ventral diencephalon extends downward to meet the superiorly migrating oral ectoderm, resulting in the formation of the neurohypophysis and adenohypophysis, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26702.s4" sec-type="Blood Supply and Lymphatics">
        <title>Blood Supply and Lymphatics</title>
        <p>The pars nervosa receives its blood supply primarily from the inferior hypophyseal arteries, with additional contribution from the superior hypophyseal artery. All these blood vessels are branches of the internal carotid artery. Hypophyseal veins provide the main venous drainage of the pars nervosa, while portal venous branches convey blood to the anterior pituitary, allowing direct effects of AVP on anterior pituitary secretory activity. Both venous systems drain into the cavernous sinuses and subsequently through the petrosal sinuses to the internal jugular vein.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26702.s5" sec-type="Physiologic Variants">
        <title>Physiologic Variants</title>
        <p>Variations in pituitary anatomy are often incidental, and several physiologic variants exist. "Empty sella syndrome" refers to an imaging appearance in which the sella turcica is partially or completely filled with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), resulting in a flattened pituitary that may appear small or poorly visualized. Secondary causes typically correspond to pathologic pituitary insufficiency, whereas a primary etiology involves an incompetent diaphragma sellae, permitting herniation of the subarachnoid space and CSF into the sella turcica, frequently in association with elevated intracranial pressure. This herniation can compress and flatten the pituitary against the sellar floor and stretch the pituitary stalk. Consequently, imaging often demonstrates an empty sella turcica, while the pituitary tissue and its function remain largely preserved.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26702.s6" sec-type="Surgical Considerations">
        <title>Surgical Considerations</title>
        <p>The most common indications for pituitary surgery are space-occupying sellar lesions of various etiologies. Tumors arising from the pars nervosa are rare compared with those of the anterior pituitary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r9">[9]</xref> The close proximity of the anterior and posterior pituitary results in overlapping surgical access, risks, and considerations, although some posterior pituitary tumors can be more vascular and present different operative challenges than typical pituitary adenomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Surgical access to the pituitary is generally achieved via a transsphenoidal approach, with transcranial approaches serving as an alternative option.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r11">[11]</xref> Microsurgical and endoscopic techniques are selected at the discretion of the surgeon, with endoscopic approaches increasingly preferred due to enhanced visualization.</p>
        <p>Common complications of pituitary surgery include SIADH,&#x000a0;AVP deficiency (AVP-D), hypopituitarism, electrolyte imbalances, hemorrhage, postoperative fever, and bacterial meningitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Postoperative water balance disorders typically follow a predictable time course.&#x000a0;Early diabetes insipidus often occurs within the first 24 hours and is frequently transient, whereas delayed hyponatremia usually presents several days after surgery. A triphasic pattern emerges in some patients, beginning with early diabetes insipidus, followed by an antidiuretic phase, and concluding with recurrent diabetes insipidus.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26702.s7" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>The pars nervosa&#x02019;s primary function involves the regulated secretion of oxytocin and AVP. The physiologic roles of these hormones, along with the conditions that trigger their release, provide a comprehensive depiction of the structure&#x02019;s clinical significance.</p>
        <p>Oxytocin plays a critical role in inducing uterine contractions that facilitate parturition and limit excessive postpartum blood loss. Cervical stretching during childbirth generates afferent signals that reach the hypothalamus, triggering oxytocin release into the systemic circulation. Oxytocin acts on uterine smooth muscle, inducing and strengthening contractions. Pharmacologic oxytocin is commonly administered to induce labor. During lactation, suckling produces afferent nerve impulses to the hypothalamus, stimulating oxytocin release, which activates receptors on myoepithelial cells of the lactiferous ducts, resulting in milk letdown. Although abnormal oxytocin levels have been associated with various psychiatric disorders, oxytocin is not currently used as a treatment in this context.</p>
        <p>AVP secretion responds to changes in plasma osmolality and effective circulating volume or pressure through 2 partially overlapping regulatory pathways. Small alterations in plasma osmolarity detected by osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus and lamina terminalis adjust pituitary AVP release, which modulates renal reabsorption of solute-free water in the collecting ducts and tubules. Increased water reabsorption lowers plasma osmolarity, providing negative feedback to AVP secretion. This circuit ensures precise regulation of overall plasma osmolarity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Significant reductions in effective circulating volume or arterial pressure activate baroreceptor pathways in the brainstem, resulting in increased AVP release. Elevated circulating AVP induces arteriolar smooth muscle contraction to raise blood pressure, enhances water reabsorption through renal mechanisms, and can promote procoagulant effects, including increased von Willebrand factor release via V2-mediated endothelial pathways. These processes act in concert&#x000a0;to maintain homeostasis during states of fluid deficit.</p>
        <p>Pathology of the pars nervosa, regardless of etiology,&#x000a0;involves either insufficient or excessive secretion of neurohypophyseal hormones. Although oxytocin fulfills multiple physiologic roles, abnormalities in its secretion rarely contribute directly to disease pathophysiology or treatment, though abnormal levels have been investigated in various psychiatric disorders. In contrast, disordered AVP secretion results in diabetes insipidus when insufficient and SIADH when excessive.</p>
        <p>Diabetes insipidus occurs when inadequate or ineffective AVP secretion prevents the kidney from concentrating urine properly. Typical presentation includes excessive thirst, dehydration, and hypernatremia in a patient excreting abnormally large volumes of dilute urine. Causes include pituitary AVP-D, leading to central diabetes insipidus (CDI), or renal resistance to AVP,&#x000a0;producing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (NDI).&#x000a0;Excessive water intake (primary polydipsia) represents an alternative cause of polyuria with dilute urine. Among these conditions, only pituitary AVP-D involves the pars nervosa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Suprasellar lesions, such as craniopharyngiomas, can affect the pituitary stalk and posterior pituitary, making CDI evident at diagnosis and a common postoperative complication following resection.</p>
        <p>Basal copeptin measurement aids in identifying NDI when levels are markedly elevated. If basal copeptin is not elevated, stimulated copeptin testing using hypertonic saline or arginine distinguishes AVP-D from primary polydipsia.&#x000a0;Low stimulated copeptin indicates CDI, whereas higher stimulated copeptin supports primary polydipsia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Pituitary insufficiency is commonly classified into 3 etiologic categories: traumatic, inflammatory or infectious, and oncologic. Traumatic causes include hemorrhagic or ischemic infarcts and direct pituitary damage resulting from blunt or penetrating brain injuries. Inflammatory and infectious etiologies encompass autoimmune hypophysitis, sarcoidosis, meningitis, encephalitis, and brain abscess. Oncologic pituitary injury arises from direct mass effects of adjacent malignancies or damage incurred during tumor resection or radiation therapy.</p>
        <p>Empty sella represents a radiologic finding in which the sella turcica is partially or completely filled with CSF, leading to flattening of the pituitary. Primary empty sella is frequently asymptomatic and usually involves an incompetent diaphragma sellae, allowing herniation of the subarachnoid space and CSF into the sella, often in association with elevated intracranial pressure, which compresses the pituitary. Secondary empty sella syndrome generally results from traumatic, infectious,&#x000a0;inflammatory, or oncologic causes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>SIADH develops when&#x000a0;AVP levels become abnormally or persistently&#x000a0;high due to hypothalamic dysregulation or ectopic production. The condition presents with hyponatremia and excessively concentrated urine, as AVP elevation drives maximal renal reabsorption of solute-free water.</p>
        <p>Hyponatremia increases intracellular volume, causing cellular swelling that particularly affects the central nervous system (CNS) and can result in neurologic dysfunction, coma, or death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r17">[17]</xref> Management of SIADH depends on symptom severity and the acuity of hyponatremia. Fluid restriction is typically 1st-line therapy, although nonresponse is common. Additional interventions in selected patients include increased solute intake, oral urea therapy, and&#x000a0;administration of&#x000a0;AVP receptor antagonists such as tolvaptan, combined with treatment of the underlying cause. Sodium correction may be attempted in severe or symptomatic cases but requires careful monitoring and limitation to prevent osmotic demyelination.</p>
        <p>Excess AVP in SIADH arises from diverse causes, broadly classified as&#x000a0;conditions that disrupt the hypothalamohypophyseal pathway and&#x000a0;pathologies that bypass&#x000a0;the pathway entirely. Pituitary dysregulation can result from CNS infections of various etiologies, hydrocephalus, trauma,&#x000a0;or neurologic diseases such as multiple sclerosis.</p>
        <p>Certain medications, including selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, chlorpropamide, carbamazepine, valproic acid, and morphine, can induce SIADH through mechanisms involving both the kidney and CNS. Multiple cancers, including small cell lung cancer, mesothelioma, gastrointestinal and genitourinary malignancies, and lymphomas, are capable of ectopic AVP production. Severe pulmonary infections and sarcoidosis may also trigger supraphysiologic AVP levels sufficient to cause clinically significant SIADH. Regardless of etiology, hyponatremia associated with SIADH requires treatment tailored to severity, symptomatology, and acuity to prevent neurologic complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>On noncontrast T1-weighted magnetic resonance imaging, the normal posterior pituitary frequently exhibits a small hyperintense &#x0201c;bright spot,&#x0201d; reflecting the high concentration of neurosecretory granules storing AVP in axon terminals. Loss of this bright spot is common in CDI caused by hypothalamic or pituitary stalk disease and often occurs alongside pituitary stalk thickening in conditions such as germinoma and Langerhans cell histiocytosis.</p>
        <p>In contrast, the bright spot is usually preserved in NDI because AVP synthesis and storage remain intact. The bright spot is also often preserved in primary polydipsia, making this finding supportive but not independently diagnostic. Pituitary stalk thickening warrants a focused differential diagnosis, including germinoma, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, lymphocytic hypophysitis, sarcoidosis, tuberculosis, and metastasis, and may justify interval imaging when the etiology is not immediately apparent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26702.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
